[NARRATIVE]
UID: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-PLANS-IN-REFS
TITLE: Plans and git refs
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
WIDGET: rows
PLACE: screen
STATEMENT: >>>
Which ref the canon lives on, the long-lived branch, squash merges, and the missing merge joint.

- Only a lean exists: plans may not belong on main; commit only the snapshot that led to work. No ref, tag or branch is named.
[LINK: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MEMBERSHIP-AND-COLLECTION-CONTRADICT]
> only a lean: "plans may not belong in main"; "commit only the snapshot that led to work"; no ref, tag or branch named [OPEN: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN]
- The counter-argument: the canon cannot query git, and its intended reader has no memory of yesterday.
[LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE]
> counter: the canon cannot query git; DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE's reader has no memory of yesterday [WORKS]
- Canon means committed, reviewed and ratified by merge — yet origin/main holds zero .sdoc files. Which ref the canon lives on is never said.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MEMBERSHIP-AND-COLLECTION-CONTRADICT]
> canon = committed + reviewed + ratified by merge, yet origin/main holds 0 .sdoc; which ref is never said [GAP]
- Work accumulates on one long-lived branch with no pull request during development. At a milestone it is cut into pull requests, each cut bounded by atomicity; acceptance happens at that second review.
[LINK: DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]
> DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH: no PR during development; cut the branch into PRs at a milestone, cut points bound by PR atomicity; acceptance at the second review [WORKS]
- A squash merge keeps the tree, so hashes stay valid, but destroys per-commit attribution. The pull request is the durable unit, and the fail-safe is running the fingerprint check on the tree about to become main.
[LINK: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT]
> squash keeps the tree (hash safe), destroys per-commit attribution; PR = durable unit; MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE fail safe = fp-check on the tree about to become main [WORKS]
- There is no joint before a merge: merge and rewrite fire only after the fact, the pre-push joint can be bypassed, and CI is not a joint. Every merge-time rule has nowhere to attach.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MERGE-BOUNDARY-IS-NOT-A-JOINT]
> the merge boundary has no PRE joint: git.merge|rewrite post-only, git.push pre bypassable, CI not a joint; merge-time rules have nowhere to attach [GAP]
<<<
